 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 554362,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "<figure class=\"unnumbered\">\n<img src=\"exercises/media/8210f3219f1e00a3.jpg\" alt=\"\">\n<figcaption>(credit: modification of work by Alessandro Cassini, MD, et al./The Lancet, under CC BY 4.0 license)</figcaption>\n</figure>",
   "stem_html": "<p>The graph shows the number of cases and number of attributable deaths for various types of antibiotic-resitant bacteria. The small graph at the bottom right part is the zoomed in view of the data on the bottom left part.</p>\n<p>Study the data for CRPA (carbapenem-resistant <i>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</i>) and 3GCRKP (third-generation cephalosporin-resistant <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i>). The bubbles for these two mostly overlap. Based on the data, make a claim about these bacteria.</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1090111,
     "content_html": "3GCRKP infections are more common and cause a lot more deaths compare to CRPA."
    },
    {
     "id": 1090112,
     "content_html": "3GCRKP infections are more common and cause slightly less deaths compared to CRPA."
    },
    {
     "id": 1090113,
     "content_html": "3GCRKP infections are less common and cause very few deaths compared to CRPA."
    },
    {
     "id": 1090114,
     "content_html": "3GCRKP infections are less common and cause slightly fewer deaths compared to CRPA."
    }
   ],
